<!--
    VINTF device-manifest fragment for AOSP's external (UVC/V4L2) camera provider.

    WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST HERE. The AOSP module
    hardware/interfaces/camera/provider/default:android.hardware.camera.provider-V1-external-service
    declares only `init_rc` — it ships NO vintf_fragments. So PRODUCT_PACKAGES installs the binary
    and its init .rc and nothing else, the service starts at boot, and servicemanager refuses the
    registration because the interface is not declared in the device manifest. Observed exactly
    that (bd remora-4ei.9):

        F android.hardware.camera.provider-V1-external-service:
              Error while registering ext camera provider service: -3
        F DEBUG : Abort message: 'Error while registering ext camera provider service: -3'
        ... main+769, immediately after class-hal start

    while `dumpsys media.camera` still said "Number of camera devices: 0". The provider was present,
    running-and-dying, and invisible — a failure that looks identical to "no HAL installed" unless
    you read the crash buffer.

    THE INSTANCE NAME IS `external/0`, TRAILING NUMBER INCLUDED. I first wrote `external` on the
    assumption that the /0 in the rc was an interface minor rather than part of the name, and
    checkvintf rejected it:

        ERROR: files are incompatible: The following instances are in the device manifest but not
        specified in framework compatibility matrix:
            android.hardware.camera.provider.ICameraProvider/external (@1)

    The framework matrix settles it — compatibility_matrix.202604.xml declares
    <regex-instance>[^/]+/[0-9]+</regex-instance> for ICameraProvider, so a bare `external` cannot
    match by construction. Version 1 is from the -V1- in the module name and is inside the
    matrix's declared 1-4 range.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device">
    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.camera.provider</name>
        <version>1</version>
        <fqname>ICameraProvider/external/0</fqname>
    </hal>
</manifest>
